{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2079 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bae56f23ada220b18c5bb16e623f97c1ce5e7c12a07702f2267da149ef72a24f" :edb/db-before-t 2078 :edb/db-after-t 2079 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535391 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:16:34.364Z" 13194139535391 true] [17592186156927 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" 13194139535391 false] [17592186156927 1100 "Which of the following integrals has an entire integrand that is odd about zero and opposite integration limits, so the standard odd-integrand cancellation rule applies directly?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" 13194139535391 true] [17592186156927 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." 13194139535391 false] [17592186156927 1145 "The direct odd-integrand cancellation rule applies to an integrable odd function on an interval with opposite endpoints: $\\int_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=0$.\n\nI satisfies this criterion: $4x^3$ is odd and continuous on $[-3,3]$, so $\\int_{-3}^{3}4x^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x=0$.\n\nII has an even, strictly positive integrand. Its value is\n\n$$\\int_{-2}^{2}(e^x+e^{-x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[e^x-e^{-x}]_{-2}^{2}=2(e^2-e^{-2})>0.$$\n\nIII has an odd integrand, $\\sin(2x)$, but its endpoints $-\\pi$ and $2\\pi$ are not opposites. Thus it does not satisfy the direct criterion. Nevertheless, III also has value zero because its interval spans three full periods:\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi}\\sin(2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\left[-\\frac12\\cos(2x)\\right]_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi}=-\\frac12\\cos(4\\pi)+\\frac12\\cos(-2\\pi)=0.$$\n\nTherefore, “I only” meets the stated direct odd-integrand criterion. If the question asked which integrals have value zero by any method, the answer would instead be I and III. An interval that fails the opposite-endpoint criterion can still have a zero integral by periodicity.\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention." 13194139535391 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
